   "source": "# GitHub Open-Source Repository Recommendation System\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\n**Problem.** Popularity-led discovery does not reliably surface open-source projects aligned with a developer's public technical interests. **Data.** This reduced, real-public-data run contains 5 developers, 637 repositories, and 290 owned/starred interactions collected from the GitHub REST API plus a documented public-HTML fallback after the unauthenticated core limit was reached. **Method.** Weighted training-only developer profiles feed TFâ€“IDF cosine similarity, language/topic matching, repository activity/quality, and calibrated popularity; random, popularity, language, content, and hybrid rankers are compared. **Evaluation.** Two users use chronological holdouts and three use reproducible leave-five-out; five test repositories are held out per user.\n\n**Measured result.** `content` ranked first by test NDCG@10 at **0.1224**, with Hit Rate@10 **0.60**, Recall@10 **0.16**, and sampled-catalog coverage **7.83%**. The content model's NDCG@10 (0.1224) exceeded random (0.0000), popularity (0.0678), and language-only (0.0000); the tuned hybrid did not win the test split. **Main limitation.** Five selected users with mixed temporal/non-temporal evaluation are sufficient for a reproducible engineering demonstration, not population-level claims.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Business Problem\n\nDevelopers face a discovery problem: repository popularity is easy to see, but relevance to their skills, technical interests, desired activity level, and contribution readiness is not. The business question is whether public GitHub activity and repository metadata can recommend more relevant projects than random, global-popularity, or language-only rules.\n\n- **Target user:** a developer exploring or contributing to public open source.\n- **Unit of analysis:** a developerâ€“repository candidate pair.\n- **Decision supported:** which unseen public repositories to place in the top recommendation positions.\n- **Analytical question:** how accurately can held-out public interactions be retrieved in the top K?\n- **Success criteria:** rank-aware gains over baselines, useful catalog coverage/diversity, reproducibility, and explanations grounded in calculated features.\n- **Constraints:** public data only, API limits, missing star timestamps in the documented HTML fallback, and no claim of ranking all GitHub.\n- **Ethics/privacy:** only public professional activity is used; no private activity, sensitive-trait inference, or automated contribution decisions.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Project Objectives\n\nThe objective is an auditable content-plus-quality recommender that accepts a sampled GitHub username, excludes repositories already observed in that developer's profile, ranks an eligible catalog, explains each score, and compares against random, popularity, and language baselines using leak-resistant holdouts.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Data Sources\n\nThe primary source is the official GitHub REST API (API version `2022-11-28`): repository search, public user profiles, and public starred repositories. README and contribution-file content uses GitHub's public raw-content host. After the unauthenticated core limit was reached, a narrowly scoped fallback parsed public GitHub profile/star and repository HTML for three developers; those records are explicitly sourced and never assigned fabricated timestamps or counts. The collection manifest preserves queries, failures, and provenance.\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 5. Data Limitations\n\nThis is a convenience sample, not a representative sample of GitHub. The run contains five deliberately varied public developers, the 100 most recent API-visible stars for two users, up to 60 public HTML-visible stars for three users, and a language-stratified candidate catalog. Missing byte-level language distributions and issue-label checks are retained as missing. Timestamped users receive chronological evaluation; fallback users receive reproducible leave-five-out evaluation, which is weaker and is reported separately. Stars indicate interest, not necessarily contribution intent.\n"
  },
  {
